  "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{t+2})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2t}(t+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{0}(0+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1)(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
